  "keyInsight": "Two numbers completely pin down a line: b tells you where it crosses the y-axis and m tells you its direction and steepness — so matching an equation to a graphed line is just reading those two features off the picture.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Standard coordinate plane with unit gridlines, x from -7 to 7, y from -5 to 5, axis labels italic 'x' and 'y' at the positive ends. Four double-arrowed lines in distinct colors: orange line I steepest positive (roughly slope 3, intercept ~2, exiting top near x=1), light blue line II shallowest (slope ~0.5, intercept 3), teal line III steep positive (slope ~2, intercept -3), dark blue line IV the only negative slope (~-2, intercept 3). Roman numerals sit just past each line's upper (or for IV, lower-right) endpoint in matching color. The slider-driven candidate line should render as a dashed purple line with a visible pivot dot at its y-intercept so rotation about b is obvious. Rise-over-run markers appear as a small right triangle along the clicked line with 'rise' and 'run' labels; only one line's marker shows at a time."
